<h1>(a,b)-tree</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="searchtree.html"><em>search tree</em></a> with the restrictions that all <a href="leaf.html"><em>leaves</em></a> are at the same <a href="depth.html"><em>depth</em></a> and all <a href="internalnode.html"><em>internal nodes</em></a> have between a and b <a href="child.html"><em>children</em></a>, where a and b are integers such that  2 &le; a &le; (b+1)/2.   The <a href="root.html"><em>root</em></a> may have as few as 2 children.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="searchtree.html"><em>search tree</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="btree.html"><em>B-tree</em></a> with 1 &le; a.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
After <em>Algorithms and Theory of Computation Handbook</em>,  page 4-18, CRC Press LLC.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

